### What is the guarded enum approach?

Model the states as an enum and put guarded transition methods on the entity. Each transition checks the current state and throws if the move is illegal.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public enum LoanStatus {
    APPLIED, UNDER_REVIEW, APPROVED, DISBURSED, CLOSED, REJECTED
}

public class Loan {
    private LoanStatus status = LoanStatus.APPLIED;
    private BigDecimal amount;

    public void submitForReview() {
        if (status != LoanStatus.APPLIED) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Can only submit for review from APPLIED, current: " + status);
        }
        status = LoanStatus.UNDER_REVIEW;
    }

    public void approve() {
        if (status != LoanStatus.UNDER_REVIEW) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Can only approve from UNDER_REVIEW, current: " + status);
        }
        status = LoanStatus.APPROVED;
    }

    public void disburse() {
        if (status != LoanStatus.APPROVED) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Can only disburse from APPROVED, current: " + status);
        }
        status = LoanStatus.DISBURSED;
    }

    public LoanStatus getStatus() { return status; }
}
```

### When do you upgrade to the State pattern?

Upgrade when each state carries substantial, genuinely different behaviour. If a long if-else on the status keeps appearing in many methods, or adding a new state means editing switches everywhere, the State pattern is cleaner.

```java
package com.bank.state;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface LoanState {
    void approve(LoanContext ctx);
    void disburse(LoanContext ctx);
    void reject(LoanContext ctx);
}

public class UnderReviewState implements LoanState {
    @Override
    public void approve(LoanContext ctx) {
        System.out.println("Loan approved, moving to APPROVED");
        ctx.setState(new ApprovedState());
    }

    @Override
    public void disburse(LoanContext ctx) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot disburse a loan under review");
    }

    @Override
    public void reject(LoanContext ctx) {
        System.out.println("Loan rejected");
        ctx.setState(new RejectedState());
    }
}

public class ApprovedState implements LoanState {
    @Override
    public void approve(LoanContext ctx) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Already approved");
    }

    @Override
    public void disburse(LoanContext ctx) {
        System.out.println("Disbursing loan of INR " + ctx.getAmount());
        ctx.setState(new DisbursedState());
    }

    @Override
    public void reject(LoanContext ctx) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot reject an approved loan");
    }
}
```

### How do you decide between guarded enum and State pattern?

| Aspect | Guarded Enum | State Pattern |
|---|---|---|
| States have | Different transitions only | Rich, different behaviour |
| Complexity | Low | Higher (more classes) |
| New state means | Edit the entity | Add a new class |
| Switch statements | Acceptable if few | Signal to upgrade |
| Best for | Simple 3-5 state lifecycle | Complex workflow with per-state logic |

```java
// Guarded enum: simple, few states, thin behaviour
loan.approve(); // guard + status change

// State pattern: rich behaviour per state
loan.approve(); // delegates to current state object
```

### When do you use inheritance vs composition for type variants?

Prefer composition over inheritance. Inheritance is appropriate only for a genuine is-a relationship with meaningful shared behaviour that honours Liskov Substitution.

```java
// Inheritance: tempting but rigid
public class HomeLoan extends Loan { } // single inheritance, tight coupling
public class CarLoan extends Loan { }  // what about a home + car combo?

// Composition: flexible, preferred
public class Loan {
    private final LoanType type;           // value object
    private final InterestStrategy interest; // pluggable strategy

    public BigDecimal calculateInterest(int days) {
        return interest.calculate(getAmount(), days);
    }
}

public enum LoanType { HOME, CAR, PERSONAL }
```

| Aspect | Inheritance | Composition |
|---|---|---|
| Flexibility | Fixed at compile time | Changeable at runtime |
| Combinations | Awkward (Java single inheritance) | Mix freely |
| Coupling | Subclass tightly coupled to superclass | Loose coupling via interface |
| Testing | Must test whole hierarchy | Test each piece independently |

### Why should entities own their state transitions?

The **anaemic domain model** anti-pattern puts entities as getters and setters and puts all logic in a fat service. This scatters business rules and lets callers bypass guards.

```java
// Anaemic model (bad): service sets status directly
public class LoanService {
    public void approveLoan(Loan loan) {
        loan.setStatus(LoanStatus.APPROVED); // no guard, any status can be set
    }
}

// Rich domain model (good): entity owns its guard
public class Loan {
    public void approve() {
        if (status != LoanStatus.UNDER_REVIEW) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot approve from " + status);
        }
        status = LoanStatus.APPROVED;
    }
}
```

### What does a thin service look like?

The service orchestrates the use case but does not contain business rules. It loads the entity, calls a behaviour, saves it, and publishes an event.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Loan;
import com.bank.repository.LoanRepository;
import com.bank.event.EventPublisher;

public class LoanService {
    private final LoanRepository repo;
    private final EventPublisher publisher;

    public LoanService(LoanRepository repo, EventPublisher publisher) {
        this.repo = repo;
        this.publisher = publisher;
    }

    public void approveLoan(String loanId) {
        Loan loan = repo.findById(loanId);
        loan.approve(); // entity enforces its own guard
        repo.save(loan);
        publisher.publish(new LoanApprovedEvent(loanId));
    }
}
```

### What are the common mistakes?

The first mistake is exposing setters for the status field. This lets any caller bypass the guards.

```java
// Wrong: public setter bypasses all guards
public void setStatus(LoanStatus status) {
    this.status = status; // anyone can set any status
}

// Correct: no setter, only guarded transition methods
public void approve() { /* guard + transition */ }
```

The second mistake is putting business rules in the service instead of the entity, which distributes the same guard logic across multiple services.

### How does refactoring work between approaches?

Because the service only calls high-level methods like `approve()`, refactoring the internal state implementation from a guarded enum to the State pattern does not require any change to the service.

```java
// Before: guarded enum inside Loan
loan.approve(); // checks status == UNDER_REVIEW, sets APPROVED

// After: State pattern inside Loan
loan.approve(); // delegates to currentState.approve(this)

// Service code is IDENTICAL in both cases
loanService.approveLoan("LOAN001"); // no change
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Entities owning transitions pairs naturally with persistence: you load the entity, invoke a behaviour, and save it. The entity guarantees it is never in an illegal state. At National Bank of India, a fat loan service once set a status with a setter and disbursed a loan that had never been approved, causing a real financial incident. Pankaj refactored so the loan entity owned guarded transitions, used a composed loan-type value instead of a subclass hierarchy, and kept the service thin. The illegal jump became impossible.
